=begin pod :kind("Type") :subkind("class") :category("basic")

=TITLE class RakuAST::Type::Parameterized

=SUBTITLE Represent a Raku type with parameterization

    class RakuAST::Type::Parameterized { }

The C<RakuAST::Type::Parameterized> class represents a Raku type with
parameterization.

=head2 Object introspection

C<RakuAST::Type::Parameterized> object is typically created when parsing
Raku Programming Language code.  So most developers will only need to
know how to introspect the objects created.

=head3 args

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
dd $type.args;  # Str
=end code

The C<args> method returns the
L<C<RakuAST::ArgList>|/type/RakuAST/ArgList> object that was specified
with the (possibly implicit) "args" named argument in the call to C<.new>.

=head3 base-type

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
dd $type.base-type;  # Int
=end code

The C<base-type> method returns the C<RakuAST::Type> object that was
specified with the "base-type" named argument in the call to C<.new>.

=head3 is-coercive

=begin code :preamble<my $type>
say $type.is-coercive;  # True or False
=end code

The C<is-coercive> method returns whether the base-type is coercive.

=head2 Object creation

There is only one way to create a C<RakuAST::Type::Parameterized> object.

=head3 new

=begin code
my $type = RakuAST::Type::Parameterized.new(
  base-type => RakuAST::Type::Simple.from-identifier("Int"),
  args      => RakuAST::ArgList.new(
                 RakuAST::Type::Simple.from-identifier("Str")
               )
);
dd $type;  # Int(Str)
=end code

The C<.new> method creates a C<RakuAST::Type::Parameterized> object. It
expects the following named arguments:

=item :base-type - RakuAST::Type object for the base type (required)
=item :args - RakuAST::ArgList object (optional, default: no arguments)

=end pod
